@prefix cell: <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/cell#> .
@prefix topic: <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/topic#> .
@prefix cat: <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/category#> .
@prefix p: <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/persona#> .
@prefix o: <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/organization#> .
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix sh: <http://www.w3.org/ns/shacl#> .
@prefix dc: <http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/> .
@base <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/cell> .

<http://mee.foundation/ontologies/cell> rdf:type owl:Ontology ;
    owl:versionIRI <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/cell/3.35.0> ;
    dc:date "2026-08-27"^^xsd:date ;
    owl:imports <http://mee.foundation/ontologies/topic> ;
    rdfs:label "Cell Ontology"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """Cell Ontology for the Mee Identity Agent. A cell is the atomic unit of the user's tree: a filesystem folder holding exactly one cell-databook file, folder and file together forming one tree node. Defines cell:Cell — the self-contained content facet of a cell, carried by that cell-databook file's own triples. cell:Cell stores no property recording the cell's own tree position: that position is simply whichever folder the cell-databook currently lives in, not a fact this RDF individual needs to carry. cell:Cell splits into two disjoint kinds: cell:TCell (a reusable, class-level template) and cell:ACell (an actual cell instantiated in a user's own tree) — a cell is always exactly one of the two, never both."""@en .


#################################################################
#    Annotation Properties for Cell DataBook Classification
#################################################################

cell:abstract rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:domain owl:Class ;
    rdfs:range xsd:boolean ;
    rdfs:label "abstract"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "True if this class is not directly instantiated in DataBooks — it exists only to organize the class hierarchy."@en .

cell:label rdf:type owl:AnnotationProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:Cell ;
    rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "label"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Default display name for a concrete cell:Cell subtype (e.g. \"2 Member Cell\"), asserted directly on the class rather than an instance."@en .


#################################################################
#    Cell
#
#    A cell is the atomic unit of the user's tree: a filesystem folder
#    holding exactly one cell-databook file, folder and file together
#    forming one tree node — cells nest inside cells, forming the tree.
#    cell:Cell is the self-contained content record carried by that
#    cell-databook file's own triples. It stores no property recording
#    the cell's own tree position: that position is simply whichever
#    folder the cell-databook currently occupies, not a fact this
#    individual needs to carry — moving the folder is a pure
#    filesystem operation, asserting nothing new on the cell itself.
#    The folder<->cell-databook relationship is strictly one-to-one,
#    not many-to-one: a folder holds at most one cell-databook file,
#    whose filename must be an exact copy of the folder's own name —
#    i.e. a folder either IS a cell (it holds exactly one
#    cell-databook) or it is a plain filesystem folder that is not a
#    cell at all, possibly with nested cells of its own further down.
#    There is no way for two cell:Cell individuals to share one
#    folder: a cell is self-contained, and letting two cells share a
#    folder would risk a single file in that folder becoming
#    ambiguously part of both.
#
#    cell:Cell itself carries only what's common to every cell
#    regardless of facet: inherent classification (cell:origin).
#    Everything facet-specific — including cell:memberTopics/
#    cell:otherTopics (its linked topic:SCTopicGraph container(s))
#    — lives on cell:TCell or cell:ACell below. There is no stored
#    property recording who or what a cell's relationship is about:
#    that fact is derivable directly from cell:memberTopics/
#    cell:otherTopics (see their own comments below) rather than
#    asserted independently — a cell:subject property once existed
#    here but was removed once every real example showed its value
#    was always mechanically computable from the topics already
#    linked.
#################################################################

cell:Cell rdf:type owl:Class ;
    cell:abstract true ;
    rdfs:label "Cell"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Superclass of every cell individual, carrying only what's common regardless of kind: inherent classification (cell:origin). cell:Cell — the RDF content facet of a cell — stores no property recording its own tree position: a cell's position is simply whichever folder it (folder + this cell-databook) currently occupies, and moving that folder is a pure filesystem operation requiring no triple to change, which is what keeps a cell robust under PDN sync. cell:origin does not violate this: its range is the abstract classificatory cat:Category, not a tree position, so it records what kind of thing the cell is, never where it currently lives. Member composition (cell:memberCount, cell:OneMember/cell:MultiMember), creator metadata (cell:creator), topic links (cell:memberTopics, cell:otherTopics), and validation (cell:shape) live on the cell:ACell kind, not directly here."@en .


#################################################################
#    Cell Kinds: Template (TCell) and Actual (ACell)
#
#    A cell:Cell splits into two disjoint kinds — a cell is always
#    exactly one, never both:
#
#    cell:TCell — a reusable, class-level template. Carries
#    cell:templateShape, reachable from a cat:Category subclass via
#    cat:templateCell (category.ttl). Describes what a topic filed
#    under that category is expected to look like once instantiated.
#    Every cell:TCell individual lives in cell-templates.ttl, never in
#    a user's own tree.
#
#    cell:ACell — an actual cell instantiated in a user's own tree.
#    Carries cell:memberCount (and, by extension, cell:OneMember/
#    cell:MultiMember), cell:creator, cell:memberTopics (required, one
#    or more linked topic:SCTopicGraph containers per member in the
#    relationship — cardinality varies by member count) and
#    cell:otherTopics (at most one additional topic:SCTopicGraph
#    container beyond that baseline), and cell:shape — the
#    sh:NodeShape(s) validating this specific cell's own content
#    directly, as opposed to content a future topic filed under a
#    template category should conform to.
#
#    cell:TCell and cell:ACell are owl:disjointWith one another: an
#    individual reached via cat:templateCell (e.g. every individual in
#    cell-templates.ttl) is typed cell:TCell only, carrying
#    cell:templateShape and nothing from cell:ACell's side — it is
#    reusable template content, not itself a piece of member-classified
#    content. When Lazy Instantiation clones that template into a new
#    cell for a user's tree, the clone is a wholly separate individual,
#    typed cell:ACell (plus a concrete member class) instead.
#################################################################

cell:TCell rdf:type owl:Class ;
    cell:abstract true ;
    rdfs:subClassOf cell:Cell ;
    owl:disjointWith cell:ACell ;
    rdfs:label "Template Cell"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "A reusable, class-level cell template — one of the two disjoint kinds of cell:Cell, the other being cell:ACell. A cell:TCell individual is reachable from a cat:Category subclass via that class's cat:templateCell value (category.ttl) — it's what the app clones into a new cell when a category of that class is first instantiated into a user's tree (Lazy Instantiation). Carries cell:templateShape, linking to the sh:NodeShape(s) describing the content a topic filed under that category is expected to conform to. Most cell:TCell individuals carry no cell:templateShape value — that's the default, not an omission. Disjoint with cell:ACell: a cell:TCell individual is never also typed cell:ACell or any of its subclasses — see cell-templates.ttl, where every template individual is typed solely cell:Cell, cell:TCell."@en .

cell:templateShape rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:TCell ;
    rdfs:range sh:NodeShape ;
    rdfs:label "templateShape"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a cell:TCell individual (one reached via a cat:Category subclass's cat:templateCell, category.ttl) directly to the sh:NodeShape(s) that describe the content a topic file filed under that category is expected to conform to — e.g. ctpl:PassportTemplateCell (cell-templates.ttl) carries pshapes:PassportDocumentShape (cell-templates-shacl.ttl). Makes the shape reachable by RDF traversal alone (cat:Category -> cat:templateCell -> cell:templateShape -> sh:NodeShape), not merely by file co-location or naming convention. A template cell with no known shape (most of them) carries no cell:templateShape value. Distinct from cell:shape (cell:ACell), which validates an actual cell's own content directly rather than describing what a future topic filed under a template category should look like — though when Lazy Instantiation clones this TCell into a new ACell, this value is exactly what gets copied into that clone's cell:shape."@en .

cell:ACell rdf:type owl:Class ;
    cell:abstract true ;
    rdfs:subClassOf cell:Cell ;
    rdfs:label "Actual Cell"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "An actual cell instantiated in a user's own tree — real, member-classified content, as opposed to a reusable class-level template (cell:TCell). Carries cell:memberCount (and is further specialized by member count into cell:OneMember and cell:MultiMember — see Cell Member Composition below), cell:creator (required, exactly one value), cell:memberTopics (required, one or more topic:SCTopicGraph containers backing the cell's content — one per member in the relationship, cardinality enforced per member count), cell:otherTopics (at most one additional topic:SCTopicGraph container beyond that baseline), and cell:shape. Who or what the cell's relationship is about is not a stored fact — it is derivable directly from cell:memberTopics/cell:otherTopics: the sole cell:otherTopics topic's own subject if one is linked, else the full set of distinct subjects among cell:memberTopics (see cell:otherTopics's own comment). Every concrete member-count class (cell:OneMember, cell:TwoMember, cell:ThreePlusMember) is a subclass of cell:ACell, not cell:Cell directly. Disjoint with cell:TCell (see cell:TCell's own comment): every cell:Cell individual in a user's own tree is typed cell:ACell, never cell:TCell, which is reserved for the reusable templates in cell-templates.ttl. Even a purely organizational cell with nothing substantive to say carries a minimal stub cell:memberTopics entry (claimed by and about cell:creator's own :Self) rather than omitting member content altogether."@en .

cell:shape rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:ACell ;
    rdfs:range sh:NodeShape ;
    rdfs:label "shape"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links an actual (non-template) cell:ACell individual directly to the sh:NodeShape(s) validating that specific cell's own content — its own linked topic data — rather than describing what a topic filed under some other, template category should look like (that's cell:templateShape, on cell:TCell). Populated by copy-on-clone: when Lazy Instantiation clones a cat:Category subclass's cell:TCell (via cat:templateCell) into a new cell:ACell for a user's tree, whatever cell:templateShape value the TCell carried is copied into the new ACell's cell:shape — the same validation expectation, now attached to the concrete instance rather than the abstract template, so content later filed under that instantiated cell can actually be validated. Optional; most cells carry no cell:shape value, since most template cells carry no cell:templateShape value to copy in the first place."@en .

cell:creator rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:ACell ;
    rdfs:range [ rdf:type owl:Class ; owl:unionOf ( p:Person o:Organization ) ] ;
    rdfs:label "creator"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Identifies who created a cell's content — a named individual of p:Person or o:Organization. Required, exactly one value, since every actual cell's content is authored by someone. Mirrors the union-range pattern used by topic:claimant (topic.ttl), referencing those classes by name without owl:imports. Only actual/instantiated cells (cell:ACell) have a creator."@en .

cell:memberCount rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:ACell ;
    rdfs:range cell:ACell ;
    rdfs:label "memberCount"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links a cell:ACell individual to the concrete cell:ACell subclass it instantiates: cell:OneMember, cell:TwoMember, or cell:ThreePlusMember. Value is the class itself (e.g. cell:OneMember), not a string — the same class-value-punning pattern cell:origin uses for its own range, cat:Category. Exactly one value required, matching that individual's own rdf:type. Only actual/instantiated cells (cell:ACell) carry member composition."@en .


#################################################################
#    Cell Member Composition
#
#    An actual (cell:ACell) cell varies by how many total members
#    (the user plus zero or more others) are involved in the
#    relationship it represents: cell:OneMember (the user alone), or
#    cell:MultiMember (the user plus one or more others, further split
#    into cell:TwoMember and cell:ThreePlusMember). Both are subclasses
#    of cell:ACell, not cell:Cell directly.
#################################################################

cell:MultiMember rdf:type owl:Class ;
    cell:abstract true ;
    rdfs:subClassOf cell:ACell ;
    rdfs:label "MultiMember"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Abstract superclass of the two cell types involving more than one total member: cell:TwoMember (exactly one other member) and cell:ThreePlusMember (two or more other members, e.g. a group)."@en .

cell:TwoMember rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf cell:MultiMember ;
    rdfs:label "TwoMember"@en ;
    cell:label "2 Member Cell" ;
    rdfs:comment "A cell representing a 1:1 relationship with a specific person, organization, or other member."@en .

cell:ThreePlusMember rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf cell:MultiMember ;
    rdfs:label "ThreePlusMember"@en ;
    cell:label "3+ Member Cell" ;
    rdfs:comment "A cell representing a shared relationship with two or more other members — e.g. several people and/or organizations."@en .

cell:OneMember rdf:type owl:Class ;
    rdfs:subClassOf cell:ACell ;
    rdfs:label "OneMember"@en ;
    cell:label "1 Member Cell" ;
    rdfs:comment "A cell with no external member — plain content with no connection or group semantics."@en .


#################################################################
#    Cell Topic Links
#
#    cell:memberTopics and cell:otherTopics both have domain
#    cell:ACell and are owl:ObjectProperty. A cell not yet typed
#    cell:ACell (no real content filed under it yet) carries neither.
#
#    cell:memberTopics is the required baseline — at least one
#    topic:SCTopicGraph per member in the relationship (cardinality
#    enforced per member count by cell-shacl.ttl's per-member-class
#    shapes, not expressible as a single OWL cardinality restriction
#    here); cell:otherTopics is at most one additional topic beyond
#    that baseline, uniformly optional across all member counts.
#
#    Together these two properties are also how a consumer derives
#    who or what a cell's relationship is about, now that there is no
#    separate stored fact for it: if the cell has one cell:otherTopics
#    value, that topic's own topic:subject is the answer; otherwise
#    the answer is the full set of distinct topic:subject values found
#    among cell:memberTopics (for a cell:OneMember cell this is
#    trivially :Self; for cell:TwoMember/cell:ThreePlusMember with no
#    cell:otherTopics, it is every one of that cell's active members).
#    See README.md's Cell Ontology section for the full rule and
#    worked examples.
#################################################################

cell:memberTopics rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:ACell ;
    rdfs:range topic:SCTopicGraph ;
    rdfs:label "memberTopics"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links an actual (instantiated) cell to its required baseline of Subject-Claimant classified topic DataBooks (topic:SCTopicGraph, which has no subclasses) — at least one per member in the relationship. Cardinality is enforced per member count by cell-shacl.ttl's per-member-class shapes: exactly 1 for cell:OneMember; 2..4 for cell:TwoMember (matching the four self-vs-other combinations — self-by-self, other-by-self, other-by-other, self-by-other); 3..N for cell:ThreePlusMember (at least one per member, no upper bound). Which of the four self-vs-other cases a given value represents comes from that topic's own subject/claimant combination, not from which property linked it or from a distinct rdf:type. Together with cell:otherTopics, this is also the sole basis for deriving who or what the cell's relationship is about — see cell:otherTopics's own comment for the rule."@en .

cell:otherTopics rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:ACell ;
    rdfs:range topic:SCTopicGraph ;
    rdfs:label "otherTopics"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Links an actual (instantiated) cell to at most one additional Subject-Claimant classified topic DataBook (topic:SCTopicGraph) beyond the required cell:memberTopics baseline. Optional, at most one value (0..1), uniformly across all member counts — enforced by cell-shacl.ttl's :ACellShape (sh:class topic:SCTopicGraph, sh:maxCount 1). This cap is what makes deriving a cell's subject unambiguous: when this single value is present, its own topic:subject is who or what the cell is about, distinct from the cell's active members; when absent, the cell's subject is simply the full set of distinct topic:subject values among cell:memberTopics — i.e. its active members themselves (e.g. a cell:TwoMember cell with no cell:otherTopics is understood as being about the connection between its two members). There is no independently-asserted cell:subject property — a cell's subject is always computed this way, never stored."@en .


#################################################################
#    Cell Folders and Content (the Files Tab)
#
#    A cell IS its folder — the folder and its one cell-databook file
#    together are the cell, not two separately-associated things.
#    Since a folder holds at most one cell-databook (see the Cell
#    section above), this folder<->cell-databook mapping is always
#    unambiguous, with nothing left to store, detect divergence in, or
#    keep in sync. cell:Cell itself still stores no path back to this
#    folder — there is no RDF individual representing the folder as
#    something separate from the cell to point at.
#
#    A cell's content, shown in the app's Files tab, is everything
#    inside its folder EXCEPT:
#      1. the cell-databook file itself;
#      2. the one file named X.md (matching the folder's own name X),
#         recognized by convention as the folder note for that cell —
#         matching the Obsidian Folder Notes plugin convention, and
#         directly compatible with other PKM folder-note tools such
#         as Logseq or Foam; and
#      3. any subfolder that is itself a cell (i.e. directly contains
#         its own cell-databook file) — that subfolder is a separate,
#         descendant cell, a distinct node in the tree of cells, never
#         part of its ancestor's own content (see the cell ownership
#         boundary rule below).
#    Both (1) and (2) are naming conventions only, discovered by
#    looking inside the folder — neither is tracked by a separate
#    ontology property.
#
#    Cell ownership boundary: a subfolder is a separate, descendant
#    cell of its own — excluded from its ancestor's Files-tab content
#    — if and only if it directly contains a *.databook.md file. Every
#    cell already carries such a file — its own cell-databook, whether
#    real content or an empty placeholder — serving double duty as
#    both content holder and tree-node marker; no other marker is
#    needed. This makes ownership resolvable one folder at a time —
#    check what's directly inside a subfolder, not the whole tree of
#    cells — and robust to the user moving folders around outside the app,
#    since the marker file travels with whatever folder it's inside;
#    there is no separately recorded path to reconcile against.
#
#    Because a cell's own note and its other files share one folder,
#    the single Cells root can be opened directly as a PKM vault
#    (e.g. an Obsidian vault) without hiding anything from it: modern
#    PKM tools handle non-Markdown files natively, so there is no need
#    for a second, notes-only hierarchy kept separate from arbitrary
#    files.
#
#    A folder note's Markdown content may link to any other note
#    anywhere in the user's own tree of cells, not just within its own
#    cell's folder — the same vault-wide linking a PKM tool like
#    Obsidian supports. When a cell is shared, the note's own text
#    (including any links it contains) travels with it like any other
#    cell content, but a link's target does not: if that target lies
#    outside this cell's own boundary (i.e. it isn't part of this
#    cell's own Files-tab content or its own folder note), the
#    recipient's tree may not contain that other cell at all, or may
#    organize it completely differently, so the link simply won't
#    resolve on their side. This is an accepted limitation of
#    cross-cell linking, not a defect — nothing in this ontology
#    tracks link targets or attempts to keep them resolvable across a
#    share, consistent with the "no ontology property" treatment of
#    the folder note itself above.
#################################################################

cell:origin rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty ;
    rdfs:domain cell:Cell ;
    rdfs:range cat:Category ;
    rdfs:label "origin"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The cat:Category subclass this cell was originally instantiated as — the concrete leaf class itself (e.g. cat:Others, cat:BankingPayments), not a string, mirroring cell:memberCount's use of class-value punning as an object property's range. For one of the four templated classes (cat:Passport/cat:BirthCertificate/cat:DriversLicense/cat:MedicalAppointmentInfo), this is literally the class whose cell:TCell template was cloned into this cell (Lazy Instantiation, via category.ttl's cat:templateCell); for any other cell, it is simply the category the cell was created to represent, asserted directly with no template involved. At most one value (0..1), on any cell:Cell regardless of facet, matching cell.png's origin arrow off Cell. Absent exactly when the cell is of the UserDefined category — a category folder the user created without picking any existing cat:Category class, identified precisely by its cell-databook's filename carrying the literal (custom) disambiguator in place of an origin-derived <catType> (e.g. Friends(custom).databook.md), not by a vague 'no canonical counterpart' judgment call. This is a different fact from whether the folder's own name matches its origin's rdfs:label verbatim — see the green ('Predefined')/purple ('UserDefined') folder-name-text convention documented in APP-BEHAVIOR.md and CLAUDE.md's Check 15/10j, which only applies when an origin IS present; a UserDefined (no-origin) cell's name is trivially always 'UserDefined' text too, since there is no label to possibly match. This value is fixed at the point the cell was created — it is not re-derived from the folder's current name, so it needs no update if the folder is later moved or renamed, keeping a cell:Cell robust under PDN sync exactly as its own class comment already promises (no stored tree-position property). Purpose: when a cell is shared with another PDN member, the recipient's app can use this value, if present, as a hint for which folder in the recipient's own tree the incoming cell should be filed under — a snapshot of the sender's original classification, robust to either side's tree being organized differently. A folder's own filesystem position has no RDF representation at all — UserDefined is a filename/display convention only, never an OWL class. Range cat:Category is referenced by name without owl:imports, mirroring cell:creator's identical pattern for p:Person/o:Organization (persona.ttl/organization.ttl) — category.ttl already imports cell.ttl, so importing it back here would create a mutual import for no reason cell:origin actually needs."@en .
